const abOceanEstimate = {
  "prompt": "Since industrialisation the ocean's surface pH has dropped about 0.1 unit (8.2 → 8.1). On the log ruler, roughly what percentage more H⁺ is that?",
  "unit": "% more H⁺",
  "answer": 30,
  "factor": 1.4,
  "why": "About 30 per cent more H⁺ — the added CO₂ dissolves as carbonic acid, making it harder for corals, shellfish and plankton to build calcium-carbonate shells.",
  "context": "Acid rain tells the same log-scale story on land: pH 5.6 natural rain dipping to 4 is twenty-five times more acidic."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/foundational/acids-bases-and-ph">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is a beginner lesson in Chemistry, on the foundational track. It assumes no
    prior chemistry — no equations, no lab experience, no jargon. We start in your
    kitchen and build up one idea at a time. Every new word is explained the moment
    it appears.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what an acid and a base are, in one plain sentence each;</li>
    <li>read the pH scale and explain what one step up or down really means;</li>
    <li>predict what an acid does when it meets a metal, a carbonate, or a base;</li>
    <li>choose the right method for making a pure salt, and list its steps;</li>
    <li>work a titration calculation, and identify common ions and gases with simple tests.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Start in your kitchen</h2>
  <p>
    Vinegar stings a cut. Lemon juice dissolves the limescale in a kettle. Soap feels
    slippery. Baking soda fizzes in vinegar. Four everyday substances — and
    one idea behind them all.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="bet on the handover before you read on" widget="predict" config={abFizzPredict} />
  <p>
    Chemists sort substances by how they treat hydrogen. An <strong>acid</strong> is a
    substance that releases H⁺ ions in water. An H⁺ ion is a hydrogen atom that has
    lost its only electron, leaving just its nucleus — a single proton. That is why
    the definition that stuck (Brønsted and Lowry, 1923) calls an acid a
    <strong>proton donor</strong>: it hands protons to whatever is nearby, like a hot
    potato everyone passes on. A <strong>base</strong> is the opposite, a
    <strong>proton acceptor</strong>: it catches H⁺ out of the water. Every acid–base
    reaction is that handover — and nearly every sharp taste (acids) and slick feel
    (bases) is it happening on your tongue or skin.
  </p>
  <p>
    Water plays both roles, slightly. In pure water a few molecules pass protons to
    each other, so water always holds a little H⁺ and a little OH⁻ (hydrogen joined to
    oxygen — hydroxide). The two amounts are exactly equal. That balance is what
    "neutral" means.
  </p>

  <h2>The pH scale: a ruler squeezed to fit</h2>
  <p>
    How much acid is in a liquid? That is its <strong>concentration</strong> — how
    much of a substance is dissolved in a given volume, measured in mol/L (moles per
    litre). A <strong>mole</strong> is a chemist's counting word, like "dozen" but far
    bigger: one mole means about 6 × 10²³ particles.
  </p>
  <p>
    Real concentrations stretch from battery acid to drain cleaner across fourteen
    powers of ten, so chemistry compresses them into the <strong>pH scale</strong>,
    defined as pH = −log₁₀[H⁺]. The brackets mean "concentration of", so the formula
    says: take the H⁺ concentration and count its powers of ten. Each single step on
    scale means ten times more (or less) acid — one digit per tenfold change.
  </p>
  <Widget no="1" caption="the pH scale is a log ruler — each unit down is a tenfold acidifier" widget="plotter" config={phCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> hover along the curve and read pairs off it — pick an
    H⁺ concentration on the horizontal axis and the curve hands you the pH that goes with
    it. Notice the log ruler's trick: every tenfold change in concentration moves the
    reading by exactly one pH unit, whether you are near lemon juice at pH 2 or near oven
    cleaner at pH 13. Read the graph from right to left and watch ten times less acid
    become one step up the scale — that compression is how fourteen powers of ten fit on
    one ruler.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read the curve from right to left. Lemon juice, pH 2, holds 100,000 times the H⁺
    of neutral water; oven cleaner, pH 13–14, sits at the other end. Your blood holds
    pH 7.35–7.45, and drifting 0.4 beyond either edge is life-threatening — so blood
    carries <strong>buffers</strong>, mixtures that soak up small additions of acid or
    base and hold the number still.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="call the log ruler's jump" widget="estimate" config={abLemonEstimate} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "eating alkaline foods shifts your blood pH and
    prevents disease." The bicarbonate buffer, the lungs exhaling CO₂ and the kidneys
    excreting the excess hold arterial blood between 7.35 and 7.45 regardless of diet —
    a tomato cannot outvote them. Food can change the pH of urine, which is the system
    working, and acid-forming diets do impose a small excretory load — but the claim
    that diet alkalinises the blood fails against the buffer arithmetic.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="true or false — the alkaline-diet claim" widget="judge" config={abBufferJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> acids donate protons, bases accept them, and the pH scale
    squeezes fourteen powers of ten into a 0–14 ruler where each step means tenfold.
  </p>

  <h2>What acids do: three signature reactions</h2>
  <p>
    Acids announce themselves through a short, fixed set of reactions, each one a
    proton handover with a by-product you can see, hear, or test. One new word
    first: a <strong>salt</strong> is what is left when an acid's hydrogen is replaced
    by a metal. Table salt is just one member of a huge family.
  </p>
  <p>
    First, acids react with <strong>metals</strong> above hydrogen in the reactivity
    series — the league table of how eagerly metals give up electrons — to give a salt
    plus hydrogen gas. Zinc in hydrochloric acid fizzes, and the bubbles are hydrogen:
    Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂ — the fizzing is the classic test for it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Second, acids react with <strong>carbonates</strong> — compounds built around the
    group CO₃, found in chalk, marble and eggshells — to give a salt plus water plus
    carbon dioxide. Drop a marble chip into acid and it effervesces; bubble the gas
    through limewater (clear calcium hydroxide solution) and it turns milky.
  </p>
  <p>
    Third, acids react with bases in <strong>neutralisation</strong>: acid + base →
    salt + water, nothing else. Acids also announce themselves to dyes: blue litmus
    turns red, and universal indicator walks through the warm end of the rainbow. An
    <strong>indicator</strong> is a dye whose colour depends on acidity — a portable
    pH test.
  </p>

  <h2>What bases do — and two words people mix up</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Bases</strong> are metal oxides and hydroxides; the ones that dissolve in
    water are called <strong>alkalis</strong>. They do the mirror image of acids: they
    accept protons, neutralise acids, and turn red litmus blue. They feel slippery
    because they turn the fats of your skin into soap — exactly why they are dangerous
    to touch.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two words beginners must prise apart: <strong>strong</strong> and
    <strong>concentrated</strong>. Strength is chemistry — the fraction of molecules
    that actually donate a proton. Hydrochloric acid is <em>strong</em>: essentially
    every molecule ionises (splits into ions) in water, yet a bottle of it can be very
    <em>dilute</em>. The acetic acid in vinegar is <em>weak</em>: only about 1 in 100
    of its molecules donates at any moment, even at full strength. Concentration is
    arithmetic — how much acid per litre. A dilute strong acid can out-acid a
    concentrated weak one, and safety rules treat them differently.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="file each fact — strength or concentration" widget="sort-buckets" config={abStrengthBuckets} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "all acids are dangerous and all bases are gentle." pH 1
    acid and pH 14 base are both capable of destroying tissue; concentrated sulfuric and
    concentrated sodium hydroxide are equally feared in a lab. Corrosiveness depends on both
    pH and chemistry, not on which side of 7 a solution sits.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "neutralisation always ends at pH 7." That is exactly
    true only for a strong acid neutralised by a strong base at 25 °C. Neutralise a weak
    acid with a strong base and the salt left behind is slightly alkaline (sodium
    ethanoate solution sits above 7); neutralise a strong acid with a weak base such as
    ammonia and the endpoint lands slightly acidic. "Neutralised" means the acid and the
    base have finished reacting — not that the number 7 was reached.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="one line of the neutralisation summary is wrong" widget="spot-error" config={abNeutralSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> acids plus metals give salt plus hydrogen; plus
    carbonates, salt plus water plus carbon dioxide; plus bases, salt plus water
    only. Strong is not the same as concentrated.
  </p>

  <h2>Oxides: what burning leaves behind</h2>
  <p>
    Burn an element and the product left behind is its <strong>oxide</strong> — the
    element joined to oxygen. It reveals which side of the acid–base line the element
    lives on.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each oxide with its character" widget="match" config={abOxideMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>Metal oxides</strong> (Na₂O, CaO, MgO) are <strong>basic</strong>: they
    react with acids and, if soluble, form alkaline solutions (CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂,
    the limewater above). <strong>Non-metal oxides</strong> (CO₂, SO₂, P₄O₁₀) are
    <strong>acidic</strong>: they react with bases and dissolve in water as acids.
    CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃ is why rain is naturally slightly acidic; SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃ is
    the mechanism of acid rain from burning sulphur-rich fuels. In between sit the
    <strong>amphoteric</strong> oxides, ZnO and Al₂O₃, which react with <em>both</em>
    acids and bases — a handy fingerprint. A few <strong>neutral</strong> oxides (CO,
    N₂O) react with neither. And the pattern is a trend: across one row of the
    periodic table, Na₂O → Al₂O₃ → SO₃ runs from basic to amphoteric to strongly
    acidic.
  </p>

  <h2>Making a salt: choosing your route</h2>
  <p>
    Acid + base → salt + water sounds like it works with any acid and any base. The
    practical question is always: which starting materials, and how do I purify the
    product? The answer depends on one property of the wanted salt — whether it
    dissolves in water.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="choose the salt route by solubility" widget="branch" config={abSaltBranch} />
  <p>
    So first, the solubility rules, worth knowing cold. All sodium, potassium and
    ammonium salts dissolve. All nitrates dissolve. Most chlorides dissolve (silver
    and lead excepted). Most sulfates dissolve (barium and lead excepted). Most
    carbonates and hydroxides do not.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a <em>soluble</em> salt, react the acid with an insoluble base. To make copper
    sulfate: warm dilute sulfuric acid, stir in black copper oxide until no more
    dissolves, filter off the excess, then evaporate the blue solution until crystals
    form. Done.
  </p>
  <p>
    For an <em>insoluble</em> salt, mix two soluble solutions whose ions swap partners
    into the wanted solid. Barium chloride plus sodium sulfate gives barium sulfate as
    a white <strong>precipitate</strong> — a solid that forms when two solutions meet.
    Filter, wash, dry: this route is <strong>precipitation</strong>. (That same salt
    is the "barium meal" lighting up a patient's gut on an X-ray — safe because it
    will not dissolve.)
  </p>
  <p>
    The third route, alkali + acid, suits sodium and potassium salts, whose bases are
    soluble. But both solutions are colourless and the endpoint invisible — so it
    needs the next technique.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> pick the route by solubility — an insoluble base for
    soluble salts, precipitation for insoluble ones, measured neutralisation for
    sodium and potassium salts.
  </p>

  <h2>Titration: measuring with a colour flip</h2>
  <p>
    Good chemistry starts with good design: fixed volumes, controlled temperature,
    one measured outcome, repeats for reliability. The flagship measurement is the
    <strong>titration</strong>. Run the unknown solution into a measured volume of the
    other from a <strong>burette</strong> — a long graduated tube reading to 0.1 cm³
    — until an indicator (phenolphthalein, methyl orange) flips colour. The endpoint
    tells you exactly how much of each reacted.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then arithmetic finishes the job with moles = concentration × volume. Worked
    example: 25.0 cm³ of NaOH at 0.10 mol/dm³ is neutralised by 20.0 cm³ of HCl. How
    strong is the acid?
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Step 1. Moles of NaOH = 0.025 dm³ × 0.10 mol/dm³ = 0.0025 mol.</li>
    <li>Step 2. The equation HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O is 1:1, so moles of HCl = 0.0025 mol.</li>
    <li>Step 3. Concentration = 0.0025 ÷ 0.020 dm³ = 0.125 mol/dm³.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    It is the neutralisation stoichiometry from the last section, with a colour
    signal for when to stop.
  </p>

  <h2>Separating, and identifying</h2>
  <p>
    A small physical toolbox does the separating. <strong>Filtration</strong> catches
    an insoluble solid out of a liquid. <strong>Evaporation and
    crystallisation</strong> recover a dissolved solid as crystals.
    <strong>Simple distillation</strong> boils off and collects the solvent — seawater
    becomes pure water plus salt. <strong>Fractional distillation</strong> separates
    two liquids by boiling point — how industry purifies ethanol and splits crude oil
    into fuels.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Paper chromatography</strong> separates dissolved dyes by how strongly
    they cling to the paper versus travel with the solvent. Each substance's
    fingerprint is its R<sub>f</sub> value: distance moved by substance ÷ distance
    moved by solvent. Comparing them is how ink forgery and doping tests begin.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, <strong>identifying ions and gases</strong> is detective work with
    chemical tells. Flame tests: sodium yellow, potassium lilac, copper blue-green.
    Sodium hydroxide solution: iron(III) ions drop a rust-brown precipitate,
    copper(II) ions a pale blue one. Silver nitrate reveals the halides: chloride
    white, bromide cream, iodide yellow. Gases: hydrogen squeaks with a lit splint,
    oxygen relights a glowing one, carbon dioxide turns limewater milky, ammonia
    turns damp red litmus blue.
  </p>

  <h2>A whole preparation, end to end</h2>
  <p>
    Now assemble the toolkit into one practical: making magnesium sulfate — Epsom
    salts — from insoluble magnesium oxide. Warm dilute sulfuric acid. Add MgO a
    spatula at a time, stirring, until no more dissolves; the leftover solid proves
    the acid has all reacted. Filter it out. Evaporate about a third of the water,
    cool slowly so large crystals grow, filter, and dry between filter papers. Acid +
    insoluble base → salt + water, executed as glassware.
  </p>
  <p>
    Compare the sodium-salt route, where design matters. Neutralising NaOH with HCl
    has no visible endpoint — both solutions are clear. So run a trial titration with
    phenolphthalein to find the neutralisation volume, then repeat
    <em>without</em> indicator (or with the barest minimum) to make the salt
    solution. Trial run, controlled volumes, repetition, a note of which error
    dominates — burette reading or endpoint overshoot. Experimental design, in one
    afternoon.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>One solution has pH 3, another pH 5. How many times more H⁺ does the first hold?</li>
    <li>An acid fizzes with a marble chip. Which gas is that, and how do you test for it?</li>
    <li>25.0 cm³ of NaOH at 0.20 mol/dm³ is neutralised by 50.0 cm³ of HCl. What is the acid's concentration?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) One hundred times — two pH steps, each a tenfold
    change: 10 × 10 = 100. (2) Carbon dioxide — bubble it through limewater, which
    turns milky. (3) Moles of NaOH = 0.025 × 0.20 = 0.005 mol; the equation is 1:1,
    so moles of HCl = 0.005; concentration = 0.005 ÷ 0.050 = 0.10 mol/dm³.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>An acid donates protons (H⁺ ions); a base accepts them — every acid–base reaction is that handover.</li>
    <li>pH is a log ruler: each one-step move on the scale is a tenfold change in acid concentration.</li>
    <li>Strong is not the same as concentrated: strength is chemistry (how fully molecules ionise), concentration is arithmetic (amount per litre).</li>
    <li>Acids + metals give salt + hydrogen; + carbonates give salt + water + carbon dioxide; + bases give salt + water only.</li>
    <li>Choose the salt route by solubility: an insoluble base for soluble salts, precipitation for insoluble ones, measured titration for sodium and potassium salts.</li>
    <li>A titration turns a colour flip into a concentration through moles = concentration × volume.</li>
    <li>Buffers soak up small additions of acid or base — which is why your blood holds pH 7.35–7.45 whatever you eat.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Two neighbours in this Chemistry track hold the foundations under this lesson: atomic
    structure shows what an H⁺ ion actually is, and the mole and stoichiometry lesson
    supplies the moles = concentration × volume arithmetic every titration runs on. Both
    return the moment any later lesson dissolves something in water.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The pH number hides an equilibrium. Water auto-ionises: 2H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺ + OH⁻, with the
    product of the two concentrations fixed at 10⁻¹⁴ at 25 °C (the constant
    K<sub>w</sub>). That single constraint is why pH 7 is neutral (H⁺ = OH⁻ = 10⁻⁷), why
    the two scales mirror each other (pH + pOH = 14), and why the neutral point
    <em>drifts with temperature</em> — pure water at 100 °C is pH ≈ 6.14, still neutral,
    because K<sub>w</sub> has moved. Weak acids add a second layer: their ionisation is an
    equilibrium with a constant K<sub>a</sub>, whose logarithmic twin pKa is the standard
    measure of acid strength used across biochemistry — the pKa of an amino acid's side
    chain decides what shape a protein takes at pH 7.4. Buffers work here too: a weak acid
    plus its conjugate base absorbs added H⁺ or OH⁻ in near-zero-net sum, which is how
    blood (CO₂/bicarbonate, plus proteins), seawater (carbonate) and shampoo (citrate)
    hold their pH against insult.
  </p>
  <p>
    The planetary case is the ocean. Since industrialisation the sea's surface pH has
    shifted from about 8.2 toward 8.1 — a 0.1 unit drop, which on the log ruler is
    <strong>30% more H⁺</strong>. The added CO₂ dissolves as carbonic acid, and the
    resulting carbonate chemistry makes it harder for corals, shellfish and plankton to
    build calcium-carbonate shells — quietly dissolving some pteropod shells outright.
    Acid rain tells the same log-scale story on land: natural rain is pH ≈ 5.6 (dissolved
    CO₂); polluted rain dips to 4 — twenty-five times more acidic, enough to acidify lakes
    and strip forest soils, and the reason SO₂ scrubbers on power-station chimneys were
    a clear regulatory win. One ruler, fourteen orders of magnitude,
    two planetary-scale consequences — the compression is the point.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="call the ocean's log-scale shift" widget="estimate" config={abOceanEstimate} />
  <p>
    The everyday case closes the loop: the antacid tablet is neutralisation dosed by the
    gram. Calcium carbonate meets stomach acid roughly as CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O +
    CO₂ — one mole of carbonate soaking up two moles of acid. A 500 mg tablet therefore
    supplies about 0.005 mol of CaCO₃, enough to neutralise about 0.01 mol of HCl — of
    the order of 100 cm³ of the stomach's 0.1 mol/L acid. Same stoichiometry as the
    titration above, packaged for the pharmacy shelf, and the fizz is the reaction's
    carbon dioxide announcing the proton handover.
  </p>

